---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Person',
  name: 'Michel Modesto',
  jobTitle: 'Fullstack developer',
  url: 'https://opsprimer.com/about/',
  knowsAbout: ['CRM', 'Marketing automation', 'API integration', 'Business operations'],
};
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About — OpsPrimer"
  description="Michel Modesto, fullstack developer. Why a developer writes about CRMs, and the rules this site follows."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    I'm Michel Modesto, a fullstack developer. For ten years I've built APIs,
    integrations and automation for small companies — and, often enough, the
    whole system around them.
  </p>

  <p>
    That's an odd background for a site about CRMs, and it's exactly why I
    started one.
  </p>

  <p>
    Most writing about these tools comes from people who <em>evaluate</em> them.
    I'm usually the person who has to make them actually work: wire the CRM into
    the billing system, find out why a webhook fired twice, explain to a client
    why the "no-code" integration quietly dropped forty leads last Tuesday.
  </p>

  <p>
    A CRM gets bought as a sales decision. Six months in, it's an operations
    problem — and the things that break are boring, specific, and almost never
    mentioned on the pricing page. Field mapping that silently truncates. Rate
    limits nobody documented. A "sync" that's really a fifteen-minute poll. An
    export that loses the one custom property your reporting depended on.
  </p>

  <h2>The rules this site follows</h2>
  <table>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Tools</td><td>I only cover what I've used. Where a claim comes from documentation instead of use, the page says so.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Weaknesses</td><td>Every tool gets a stated weakness — including the ones that pay me.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Numbers</td><td>Limits and prices come from official sources, dated on the page. When they change, the page changes.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Client data</td><td>Never in a screenshot. Anything shown from a real account is a scratch workspace with fake records.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Money</td><td>Affiliate links, disclosed at the top of every article that has one. No sponsored posts, no paid placement.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Why this exists</h2>
  <p>
    Search results for this topic are mostly written by the companies selling the
    software. That isn't a conspiracy — a vendor simply can never publish the
    sentence "don't buy ours, buy theirs." I can, and I do.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p><a href="mailto:michelthedev@gmail.com">michelthedev@gmail.com</a></p>
</BaseLayout>
